
Docker Swarm
is a native container orchestration tool integrated directly into the Docker Engine that allows administrators and developers to manage a cluster of Docker hosts as a single, virtual system. In "swarm mode", multiple physical or virtual machines running Docker join together to form a cluster, providing built-in features for high availability, scaling, service discovery, and load balancing. 
Key Concepts
A Docker Swarm cluster consists of two types of nodes: 

    Manager Nodes: These nodes handle orchestration and cluster management functions, maintain the desired state of the swarm, and dispatch tasks to worker nodes. They use the Raft Consensus Algorithm to ensure a consistent internal state across the cluster.
    Worker Nodes: These nodes receive and execute the tasks assigned by the manager nodes. By default, manager nodes also run services as worker nodes, but they can be configured to be manager-only. 

Workloads are deployed as services, which are definitions of the desired state for an application (e.g., how many replicas of an image to run, which ports to publish, attached networks, etc.). The swarm manager continuously monitors the cluster and reconciles the actual state with the desired state. 
Key Features and Benefits

    Integrated with Docker Engine: Swarm mode is built into the existing Docker CLI, so users already familiar with Docker commands can easily start using orchestration features without needing to install separate tools or learn an entirely new interface.
    High Availability and Fault Tolerance: By distributing tasks across multiple manager and worker nodes, the swarm can tolerate the failure of individual nodes. If a node fails, the manager reschedules its tasks on healthy, available nodes.
    Load Balancing: Docker Swarm features an internal DNS component and an ingress routing mesh that automatically distributes incoming requests across the running containers for a given service.
    Scalability: Services can be easily scaled up or down by declaring the number of tasks (replicas) required using the docker service scale command.
    Secure by Default: All communications between nodes are secured with mutual Transport Layer Security (TLS) authentication and encryption.
    Rolling Updates: Deploying updates to services can be done incrementally without downtime. If an update fails, the system can roll back to a previous version of the service. 

When to Use Docker Swarm
Docker Swarm is ideal for small to medium-scale deployments, projects needing rapid deployment, and teams already invested in the Docker ecosystem seeking simple, native container orchestration. While robust, it is generally considered less feature-rich and less complex than Kubernetes, which is often preferred for large, complex enterprise environments with extensive customization needs. 
To get started, you can run docker swarm init on a host to create a new swarm and then use the provided join command to add worker and manager nodes. More information is available in the official Docker Docs. 
